#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184092 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184194 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "897ae010-a9a2-5971-a1ec-c2618cb8ad2c" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Population Variance From Sample Data"}] :question/id #uuid "a50a0dd2-10b1-5984-ae1b-6198de3f44ce" :question/math-academy-id "e-7880" :question/problem "Suppose the following data is sampled from a population. Compute an unbiased estimate of the population variance.\n\n$$\n5, \\quad 3, \\quad 6, \\quad 4, \\quad 7\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots X_n$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population variance $\\sigma^2.$ Then the sample variance $S^2$ is given by\n\n$$\nS^2 = \\dfrac{1}{n-1} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left( X_i - \\overline{X} \\right)^2 = \\dfrac{n}{n-1} \\left[ \\overline{X^2} - \\left( \\overline{X} \\right)^2 \\right] .\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- the sample variance $S^2$ is an unbiased estimator of the population variance $\\sigma^2,$ and\n\n- $\\hat{\\sigma}^2  = s^2$ denotes the unbiased estimate of $\\sigma^2$ computed from the sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n.$\n\nFirst, we will compute the quantity\n\n$$\n\\overline{x^2} - \\left( \\overline{x} \\right)^2.\n$$\n\nWe proceed as follows:\n\n- The mean of the data is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{5}(5+3+6+4+7) \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{5} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The mean of the squared data is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{5}({5}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{6}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{7}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{135}{5} \\\\  & =27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2} & =27-{5}^{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the sample variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{5-1}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, an unbiased estimate of the population variance is $2.5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184093 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184195 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb182688-828c-5252-8d6d-d09c93ba2bc8" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Estimate of the Population Variance Using the Alternative Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "86da2f2b-8028-5807-ba02-63722c4c0f61" :question/math-academy-id "e-7881" :question/problem "Suppose that $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_9$ is a random sample of size $n=9$ from a population, and \n\n$$\n\\overline{x^2} - \\left( \\overline{x} \\right)^2 = 4.\n$$\n\n \n\nCompute an unbiased estimate of the population variance." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots X_n$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population variance $\\sigma^2.$ Then the sample variance $S^2$ is given by\n\n$$\nS^2 = \\dfrac{1}{n-1} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left( X_i - \\overline{X} \\right)^2 = \\dfrac{n}{n-1} \\left[ \\overline{X^2} - \\left( \\overline{X} \\right)^2 \\right] .\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- the sample variance $S^2$ is an unbiased estimator of the population variance $\\sigma^2,$ and\n\n- $\\hat{\\sigma}^2  = s^2$ denotes an unbiased estimate of $\\sigma^2$ that's computed from the sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n.$\n\nHere, there are $n=9$ data points in the sample, and we have\n\n$$\n\\overline{x^2} - \\left( \\overline{x} \\right)^2 = 4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, an unbiased estimate of the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{9-1}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{8} \\\\  & =4.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184112 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184208 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2aa1599e-b9c0-5282-ac58-b10211cb7d30" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Estimate of the Population Variance"}] :question/id #uuid "b3c0e272-0059-59e6-b3ea-55aaecb14cda" :question/math-academy-id "e-9921" :question/problem "A random sample of size $n=81$ is conducted from a population. It is known that\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^{81} \\left( x_i - \\overline{x} \\right)^2 = 180\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere each $x_i$ is a sample observation. Compute an unbiased estimate of the population standard deviation." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots X_n$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population variance $\\sigma^2.$ Then the sample variance $S^2$ is given by\n\n$$\nS^2 = \\dfrac{1}{n-1} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left( X_i - \\overline{X} \\right)^2 = \\dfrac{n}{n-1} \\left[ \\overline{X^2} - \\left( \\overline{X} \\right)^2 \\right] .\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- the sample variance $S^2$ is an unbiased estimator of the population variance $\\sigma^2,$ and\n\n- $\\hat{\\sigma}^2  = s^2$ denotes an unbiased estimate of $\\sigma^2$ that's computed from the sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n.$\n\nWe're given that $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{81} \\left( x_i - \\overline{x} \\right)^2 = 180$ and $n=81.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{81-1}\\cdot 180 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{80}\\cdot 180 \\\\  & =2.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, an unbiased estimate of the population variance is $\\hat{\\sigma}^2  = 2.25.$ We take the square root to compute an unbiased estimate of the standard deviation. This gives\n\n$$\n\\hat{\\sigma} = \\sqrt{2.25} = 1.5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443487 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184208 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2aa1599e-b9c0-5282-ac58-b10211cb7d30" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Estimate of the Population Variance"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d937d0f-642b-5b54-95f1-386570968e23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443416} {:answer/id #uuid "5f99ee14-f921-55bb-beae-94d55f4763f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443429} {:answer/id #uuid "7682beb4-209c-5c8e-b02c-5574fd9c52a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443436} {:answer/id #uuid "a8bb74fe-3173-5959-9647-a075edc932ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443441} {:answer/id #uuid "d6f4d205-ccff-5bbf-b86d-955d5121dea6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443455}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f99ee14-f921-55bb-beae-94d55f4763f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443429} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a76954e-73f1-5964-bd22-6c22516b62a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443470}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c0d91061-b2d9-57bd-a897-ea0b99d019a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-139847" :question/problem "A random sample of size $n=11$ is conducted from a population. It is known that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{11}{({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}=220\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere each ${x}_{i}$ is a sample observation. Compute an unbiased estimate of the population variance." :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ Then, the sample variance ${S}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{S}^{2}=\\frac{1}{n-1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}=\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{X}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}].\n$$\n\nNote that:\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- the sample variance ${S}^{2}$ is an unbiased estimator of the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ and\n\n- ${\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2}={s}^{2}$ denotes an unbiased estimate of ${\\sigma }^{2}$ that's computed from the sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}.$\n\nWe're given that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{11}{({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}=220$ and $n=11.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{11-1}\\cdot 220 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot 220 \\\\  & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, an unbiased estimate of the population variance is $22.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443478 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184208 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2aa1599e-b9c0-5282-ac58-b10211cb7d30" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Estimate of the Population Variance"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15dde39d-88be-58d2-9cfe-766d0c016346" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443415} {:answer/id #uuid "26f31e5b-1fa1-56d2-8786-d2c285ab5fa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.68" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443418} {:answer/id #uuid "74e5d5fc-d551-5446-8f5a-277c7dc3e028" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443435} {:answer/id #uuid "90b3402d-6afa-58de-87a4-ace03f1b7710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443438} {:answer/id #uuid "f3562734-54c8-5167-889c-5d3f14bb8dea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443463}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90b3402d-6afa-58de-87a4-ace03f1b7710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443438} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba1e9e1a-855b-5e0e-8730-feb174aced84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443477}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1f7a0341-d787-5ede-9a89-8dbc8c2dee11" :question/math-academy-id "q-139856" :question/problem "A random sample of size $n=51$ is conducted from a population. It is known that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{51}{({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}=24.5\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere each ${x}_{i}$ is a sample observation. Compute an unbiased estimate of the population standard deviation. Round your answer to two decimal places where appropriate." :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ Then, the sample variance ${S}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{S}^{2}=\\frac{1}{n-1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}=\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{X}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}].\n$$\n\nNote that:\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- the sample variance ${S}^{2}$ is an unbiased estimator of the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ and\n\n- ${\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2}={s}^{2}$ denotes an unbiased estimate of ${\\sigma }^{2}$ that's computed from the sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}.$\n\nWe're given that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{51}{({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}=24.5$ and $n=51.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{51-1}\\cdot 24.5 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}\\cdot 24.5 \\\\  & =0.49.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, an unbiased estimate of the population variance is ${\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2}=0.49.$ We take the square root to compute an unbiased estimate of the standard deviation. This gives\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\sigma }=\\sqrt{0.49}=0.7.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443482 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184195 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb182688-828c-5252-8d6d-d09c93ba2bc8" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Estimate of the Population Variance Using the Alternative Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4df8442f-f169-58a3-b714-41e6cbd06201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "210" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443424} {:answer/id #uuid "51fdafc4-a887-576d-9d44-dc49982d6b64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "207" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443425} {:answer/id #uuid "58d652cf-3429-5aa2-a2ee-7be062fe130d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "195" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443427} {:answer/id #uuid "62a2cb5c-ef53-5b31-84fc-b239e98b35e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443430} {:answer/id #uuid "ad722f71-6f2c-5852-b0ba-376a157eab4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "198" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62a2cb5c-ef53-5b31-84fc-b239e98b35e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "